    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d939">

                <pb facs="0779" n="1979" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v07p2/pg1979"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.0221/11–454: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 939<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom
                            (Aldrich) to the
                            Department of State</hi><anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d939-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-11-04T11:00:00+00:00">November 4, 1954—11
                        a.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2205. Eyes only Secretary. Reference Embassy telegrams 2122<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d939-f2"/> and 2167<anchor n="3" xml:id="d939-f3"/> (repeated
                    information Paris 302 and 307, Rome 254 and 250 and Vienna 53 and 54).</p>
                <p>Foreign Office advised Embassy yesterday that HMG
                    could not augment its forces in Austria by use of troops released from Trieste
                    or otherwise. Usual reasons were cited, i.e., need for troops elsewhere, burden
                    which requisitioning quarters would impose on Austrian economy and political
                    problem this would create for UK in its relations with Austria, financial
                    difficulty, and difference, as Foreign Office understands it, in US and UK
                    military views concerning role of forces in Austria and their deployment there
                    for strategic purposes.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d939-f4"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Aldrich</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d939-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d939-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Paris, Vienna, and Rome eyes only for the Ambassadors.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d939-f2" n="2" xml:id="d939-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 2122 reported that Eden would
                    talk with Macmillan about this matter to
                    find out what his final decision was. (763.0221/10–2954)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d939-f3" n="3" xml:id="d939-f3-content">
                <p>Telegram 2167 reported Macmillan’s
                    statement that he would be talking with Eden on Nov. 2 and that the matter would be discussed by the
                    Cabinet on Nov. 3. Once the decision had been made, Macmillan agreed to inform Aldrich immediately. (763.0221/11–254)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d939-f4" n="4" xml:id="d939-f4-content">
                <p>Excerpts from letters sent to Ambassador Aldrich by both Eden and
                        Macmillan expressing regret that the
                    British Government was not able to meet the wishes of the U.S. Government on
                    this issue were transmitted to the Department of State in telegram 2318 from
                    London, Nov. 12. (763.0221/11–1254)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
